{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's 'pragmatic wager' defense fails because it smuggles in induction by implicitly assuming that 'surviving severe tests' is a reliable indicator of future 'fitness.' Popper explicitly rejects the idea that past survival confers any positive status or probability of future success; therefore, claiming it is the 'only rational choice' presupposes that the future will resemble the past regarding theory performance, which is the very inductive principle the target claims to avoid.","premise":"Popper holds that corroboration reports only past performance and implies nothing about future reliability or truth likelihood.","premises_essential":["5569474a29b214595e4f7f9a7ee562f44371251bace6db4deb89a1b70e0c2f3d"],"successor_question":"If past survival indicates no future fitness, on what non-inductive basis can one claim preferring the best-tested theory is 'rational' rather than arbitrary?","target":"5569474a29b214595e4f7f9a7ee562f44371251bace6db4deb89a1b70e0c2f3d"}]}